## TL;DR — Asscher Cut Diamond Size Chart

**Core finding:** A 1 carat [asscher cut diamond](/asscher-cut-diamond) measures approximately 5.5×5.5mm face-up — about 9% less surface area than a 1ct round brilliant at 6.5mm. That gap is visible without a loupe.

**Why it happens:** The asscher carries more carat weight in depth, not spread. Step-cut geometry requires a deeper pavilion to produce the hall-of-mirrors effect the shape is famous for. You pay for that depth in carat weight without seeing it from above.

**Best natural entry:** → [GIA 1.00ct E-VS1 at $2,464](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

**Best lab entry for size:** → [IGI 1.52ct D-IF at $2,795](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991043?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — 1.5ct face-up presence at near 1ct natural price

**Priority:** If finger coverage matters more than the hall-of-mirrors effect, consider the [emerald cut vs asscher cut comparison](/emerald-cut-vs-asscher-cut-diamond) — same step-cut aesthetic, 8% more spread at the same weight.

---

The asscher cut diamond size chart tells a story most buyers aren't prepared for. You search "how big is a 1 carat asscher" expecting to see something like a 1 carat round, and instead you find a stone that measures 5.5×5.5mm — noticeably smaller than the 6.5mm round you've been picturing.

This isn't a quality problem. It's geometry. Step cuts carry more weight in depth than brilliant cuts.

The deeper the pavilion, the more carat weight sits below the girdle — in the part of the stone you cannot see from above. That depth creates the architectural light corridors the asscher is built around. But it reduces face-up spread as the price of that depth.

This post gives you the full carat-to-mm chart, the face-up area comparison against round and emerald cut, the Finger Coverage Formula for your specific ring size, and current certified price data at every size from 1ct to 3ct+. Read the chart before you set your budget — it changes what carat weight makes sense for the presence you want.

---

## Asscher Cut Diamond Size Chart: Full mm Reference

Every measurement below reflects the typical range across well-cut asscher diamonds in ideal proportion range. Individual stones vary by ±0.2mm depending on depth percentage, table size, and girdle thickness.

| Carat | mm (L×W) | Face-Up Area | Round Diameter | Face-Up vs Round |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 0.25ct | 3.5×3.5mm | 12.3mm² | 4.1mm | −8% |
| 0.33ct | 4.0×4.0mm | 16.0mm² | 4.4mm | −9% |
| 0.50ct | 4.5×4.5mm | 20.3mm² | 5.0mm | −3% |
| 0.75ct | 5.0×5.0mm | 25.0mm² | 5.8mm | −5% |
| 1.00ct | 5.5×5.5mm | 30.3mm² | 6.5mm | −9% |
| 1.25ct | 5.8×5.8mm | 33.6mm² | 6.9mm | −10% |
| 1.50ct | 6.1×6.1mm | 37.2mm² | 7.4mm | −14% |
| 1.75ct | 6.4×6.4mm | 41.0mm² | 7.8mm | −14% |
| 2.00ct | 6.6×6.6mm | 43.6mm² | 8.2mm | −17% |
| 2.50ct | 7.2×7.2mm | 51.8mm² | 8.8mm | −15% |
| 3.00ct | 7.7×7.7mm | 59.3mm² | 9.4mm | −15% |
| 3.50ct | 8.2×8.2mm | 67.2mm² | 9.9mm | −13% |
| 4.00ct | 8.5×8.5mm | 72.3mm² | 10.4mm | −15% |
| 5.00ct | 9.0×9.0mm | 81.0mm² | 11.0mm | −15% |

**What this means:** At 0.25–0.50ct, the gap vs round is smaller and barely noticeable. From 1ct up, the deficit opens to 9–17% and becomes clearly visible when comparing shapes side by side. At 2ct+, the asscher is often visually read as a 1.5ct round by buyers who haven't done this comparison.

**Verdict:** The asscher consistently shows 9–17% less face-up area than a round brilliant of the same carat weight. This gap grows at 1.5ct and above. If you're shopping by finger coverage, size up by at least 0.25–0.50ct to match the presence of a round at your target size.

