## The Pavilion Weight Penalty

The asscher cut carries a hidden tax that never appears in the carat number on the certificate. Call it the Pavilion Weight Penalty.

Step-cut diamonds — asscher and emerald included — need a deeper pavilion than brilliant cuts to function optically. The ideal depth percentage for an asscher runs 60–68%. A well-cut round brilliant runs 59–62.5%.

That 2–6 percentage point difference sounds small. In real weight at 1ct, it means roughly 0.08–0.12ct of diamond mass sits in depth you cannot see from above. You pay for that weight in full.

It produces the hall-of-mirrors corridor effect — but it does not show up in face-up spread.

The geometric logic makes sense once you see it. The step facets need height to create the internal light corridors. The deeper the stone, the longer those corridors reflect.

A shallow asscher with a lower depth percentage measures wider face-up, but the hall-of-mirrors effect becomes compressed and less dramatic. You get more spread or more depth — you rarely get both.

The Pavilion Weight Penalty shows up hardest at 2ct. A 2ct asscher measures roughly 6.6×6.6mm. A 2ct round brilliant measures 8.2mm in diameter.

That's 9.2mm² of difference in face-up surface — about 17% more area on the round. On a size 6 finger, the round covers 48% of the finger; the asscher covers 39%. You'll see it.

This is not a reason to avoid the asscher. It's a reason to buy the right carat weight for your size goal. Most buyers targeting 2ct round presence in asscher will be happier at 2.5ct asscher than 2ct asscher.

The Pavilion Weight Penalty becomes a size upgrade recommendation, not a disqualifier.

