## How Big Is a 1 Carat Asscher Cut Diamond?

A 1 carat asscher cut diamond measures approximately 5.5×5.5mm face-up. That's just under the diameter of a pencil eraser — roughly 22% of the width of a standard US dime.

On a size 6 finger (17mm wide), a 5.5mm stone covers about 32% of the finger width. A 1ct round at 6.5mm covers 38%. You'll notice the difference if you hold both up to the light.

The depth of a 1ct asscher typically runs 3.5–3.8mm. The stone is nearly as deep as it is wide. That depth is what creates the visual chamber effect — the step facets appear to recede toward the culet like a series of mirrors in a tunnel.

At [ideal asscher proportions](/asscher-cut-diamond-ideal-proportions) (depth 60–68%, table 60–68%, L/W 1.00–1.05), a 1ct stone lands in the 5.3–5.7mm range. A shallower stone runs closer to 5.7mm but sacrifices depth. A deeper stone hits 5.3mm but delivers the full optical chamber.

Neither is wrong — they represent different priorities for the same carat weight.

