## The Finger Coverage Formula

Most buyers shop asscher cut diamonds by carat weight. The number that actually determines how the stone reads on the hand is finger coverage — what percentage of your finger width the stone fills.

Here is how to calculate it: **finger coverage % = (stone width in mm ÷ finger width in mm) × 100**

Typical finger widths by ring size: size 5 = ~15mm, size 6 = ~17mm, size 7 = ~19mm, size 8 = ~21mm.

| Stone | mm | Size 5 (15mm) | Size 6 (17mm) | Size 7 (19mm) | Size 8 (21mm) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 0.50ct asscher | 4.5mm | 30% | 26% | 24% | 21% |
| 0.75ct asscher | 5.0mm | 33% | 29% | 26% | 24% |
| 1.00ct asscher | 5.5mm | 37% | 32% | 29% | 26% |
| 1.25ct asscher | 5.8mm | 39% | 34% | 31% | 28% |
| 1.50ct asscher | 6.1mm | 41% | 36% | 32% | 29% |
| 1.75ct asscher | 6.4mm | 43% | 38% | 34% | 30% |
| 2.00ct asscher | 6.6mm | 44% | 39% | 35% | 31% |
| 2.50ct asscher | 7.2mm | 48% | 42% | 38% | 34% |
| 3.00ct asscher | 7.7mm | 51% | 45% | 41% | 37% |

**What this means:** 30–35% coverage is tasteful and proportional — typical for 0.50–0.75ct stones. 35–42% makes the stone the clear focal point — the 1–1.75ct range on most hands. 42–51% is a statement piece that commands full attention — 2ct and above on most fingers.

**Verdict:** Finger size matters more than most buyers factor in when choosing between 1ct and 1.25ct. On a size 5 finger, the jump from 1ct (37%) to 1.25ct (39%) is modest. On a size 7 finger, 1ct covers only 29% — a stone that risks looking too small for its price.

Buyers with size 7+ fingers should start at 1.25ct minimum for meaningful visual presence.

