## Asscher Face-Up Size vs Other Diamond Shapes

The asscher sits near the bottom of the shape hierarchy for face-up coverage at any given carat weight. Here is where it lands against every major shape at 1ct.

| Shape | 1ct Dimensions | Face-Up Area | vs Asscher 1ct |
|---|---|---|---|
| Marquise | ~12×6mm | ~56.5mm² | +87% larger |
| Pear | ~9.5×6.0mm | ~44.8mm² | +48% larger |
| Oval | ~9.0×6.0mm | ~42.4mm² | +40% larger |
| Round | 6.5mm diameter | ~33.2mm² | +10% larger |
| Princess | ~5.8×5.8mm | ~33.6mm² | +11% larger |
| Cushion | ~5.7×5.7mm | ~32.5mm² | +7% larger |
| Emerald | ~6.8×4.8mm | ~32.6mm² | +8% larger |
| Radiant | ~6.0×5.0mm | ~30mm² | ≈ equal |
| **Asscher** | **5.5×5.5mm** | **30.3mm²** | — |

**What this means:** Oval, pear, and marquise have 40–87% more face-up area than a same-weight asscher — a dramatic gap in perceived size on the finger. Round and princess are 10–11% larger. Emerald and radiant land closest to the asscher in raw face-up area, though the emerald's elongated shape creates more apparent length even at similar square millimeter counts.

**Verdict:** The asscher and radiant have the smallest face-up footprint among the most popular fancy shapes. If face-up size is the priority, oval and pear give you the most spread per dollar. If the step-cut look is the priority, the emerald cut gives you 8% more spread while keeping the same architectural aesthetic — but trades the asscher's square geometry for rectangular length.

