## Frequently Asked Questions

### What size is a 1 carat asscher cut diamond in mm?

A 1 carat asscher cut diamond measures approximately 5.5×5.5mm face-up. This can range from 5.3mm to 5.7mm depending on the exact depth percentage — deeper-cut stones run slightly narrower, shallower ones run slightly wider.

### Is a 1 carat asscher cut diamond smaller than a 1 carat round?

Yes, and visibly so. A 1ct round brilliant measures 6.5mm in diameter with approximately 33.2mm² of face-up area. A 1ct asscher measures 5.5×5.5mm with approximately 30.3mm² — about 9% less face-up surface.

The difference is clear when you compare both shapes side by side.

### How big is a 2 carat asscher cut diamond in mm?

A 2 carat asscher cut diamond measures approximately 6.6×6.6mm face-up. That's 83% of the face-up area of a 2ct round brilliant at 8.2mm. On a size 6 finger, a 2ct asscher covers about 39% of the finger width.

### What is the face-up area of a 1 carat asscher cut diamond?

The face-up area of a 1 carat asscher is approximately 30.3mm² (5.5×5.5mm). Compare this to 33.2mm² for a 1ct round brilliant and 32.6mm² for a 1ct emerald cut. The asscher has the smallest face-up footprint among common fancy shapes at 1ct.

### Why does the asscher cut diamond look smaller than other diamonds of the same carat weight?

The asscher carries more weight in depth than brilliant cuts. The ideal depth percentage for an asscher is 60–68%, vs 59–62.5% for a round. That extra depth holds carat weight below the girdle — in the optical chamber you pay for but can't see from above.

It produces the hall-of-mirrors effect, but it reduces face-up spread as the tradeoff.

### How do I calculate finger coverage for an asscher cut diamond?

Divide the stone's width in mm by your finger width in mm, then multiply by 100. Example: a 5.5mm asscher on a 17mm (size 6) finger = 5.5 ÷ 17 × 100 = 32% coverage. Most buyers find 32–42% creates a balanced, proportional look for an engagement ring.

### What carat asscher matches the presence of a 1 carat round diamond?

To match the face-up presence of a 1ct round at 6.5mm, you need approximately a 1.4–1.5ct asscher. A 1.52ct lab asscher at [$2,795](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991043?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) measures 6.1mm — the closest practical match to a 1ct round's 6.5mm at near-equivalent price.

### What carat asscher matches the face-up size of a 2 carat round?

A 2ct round brilliant measures 8.2mm. To match that face-up width in asscher, you'd need approximately 3.5ct in asscher (8.2mm). Most buyers targeting "2ct round presence" land at 2.5ct asscher (7.2mm) as a practical compromise — similar visual weight without needing a 3.5ct stone.

### Is a 0.50 carat asscher cut diamond too small for an engagement ring?

At 0.50ct, an asscher measures about 4.5×4.5mm — proportional on a size 5–6 finger (26–30% coverage) but modest on larger hands. Most engagement ring buyers start at 1ct minimum in asscher. The hall-of-mirrors effect needs size to fully develop — below 0.75ct, the architectural depth reads as subtle rather than dramatic.

### Does the asscher cut look bigger or smaller than the emerald cut?

The emerald cut has slightly more face-up area at the same weight — about 32.6mm² vs 30.3mm² at 1ct. The emerald's rectangular shape (typically 1.3–1.5 L/W ratio) creates more apparent length on the finger. The asscher is square (1:1 L/W), so the emerald appears both larger and longer.

See the full [emerald vs asscher breakdown](/emerald-cut-vs-asscher-cut-diamond) for the complete visual character comparison.

### What is the face-up size difference between asscher and princess cut at 1 carat?

A 1ct princess cut measures approximately 5.8×5.8mm (33.6mm²) vs a 1ct asscher at 5.5×5.5mm (30.3mm²). The princess is about 11% larger face-up. The [princess vs asscher comparison](/princess-cut-vs-asscher-cut-diamond) goes deeper on the visual differences — princess is brilliant-cut with high sparkle, asscher is step-cut with geometric depth.

### Does the royal asscher cut look the same size as a standard asscher at the same carat weight?

Yes, nearly identical. Both the [royal asscher](/royal-asscher-cut-diamond) (74 facets) and standard asscher (58 facets) are square step cuts with similar depth and proportion ranges. At the same carat weight and depth percentage, the face-up footprint is essentially equal.

The visual difference is in the facet pattern and sparkle character, not the size.

### Should I buy a larger carat or upgrade the clarity grade in asscher?

Always buy larger over clarity-upgrading, unless your current clarity has visible inclusions. Going from 1ct E-VS1 at [$2,464](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) to D-VVS2 at [$6,072](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/9160514?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) adds $3,608 for an upgrade no one sees without magnification. That same $3,608 puts you into a 1.5ct+ stone with meaningfully more face-up presence.

